    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "d321741a_69be22e3",
        "filename": "src/gpu/graphite/geom/Geometry.h",
        "patchSetId": 20
      },
      "lineNbr": 85,
      "author": {
        "id": 8341
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-07-19T18:16:22Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This can be restructured ti follow setShape and setSubRun if we use `sk_sp\u003cSkVertices\u003e` for fVertices.\n\n    void setVertices(sk_sp\u003cSkVertices\u003e vertices) {\n        if (fType \u003d\u003d Type::kVertices) {\n            fVertices \u003d std::move(vertices);\n        } else {\n            this-\u003esetType(Type::kVertices);\n            new (\u0026fVertices) sk_sp\u003cSkVertices\u003e(std::move(vertices));\n        }\n    }\n    \nI think this should solve the issues you mentioned where we were trying to unref garbage. When `fType` was alread kVertices, we know the original value of `fVertices` is a valid `sk_sp`, so the assignment will do the correct thing between fVertices and vertices. However, in the else block, after we call setType(), we know fVertices is in an undefined state because the old contents had been something other than an sk_sp. Doing an in-place initialization constructor will set fVertices to a valid state without trying to unref or destruct the old value of fVertices.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 80,
        "startChar": 8,
        "endLine": 85,
        "endChar": 9
      },
      "revId": "7ed706a93e568df346c5ec6b33d2a9264ac93674",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "b88aa82f_85644851",
        "filename": "src/gpu/graphite/geom/Geometry.h",
        "patchSetId": 20
      },
      "lineNbr": 123,
      "author": {
        "id": 8341
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-07-19T18:16:22Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "If `fVertices` was updated to be `sk_sp\u003cSkVertices\u003e`, we can change this to\n\n    } else if (isVertices() \u0026\u0026 type !\u003d Type::kVertices) {\n        fVertices.~sk_sp\u003cSkVertices\u003e();\n    }\n    \nWhich is basically the same as unreff\u0027ing it, but keeps it consistent with Shape and SubRun\u0027s cleanup. We don\u0027t need the 2nd else-if block if we then change how setVertices works.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 119,
        "startChar": 9,
        "endLine": 123,
        "endChar": 9
      },
      "revId": "7ed706a93e568df346c5ec6b33d2a9264ac93674",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
